      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The Very Large Telescope (VLT) is located on Cerro Paranal, Chile at an altitude of about 2.6 km above sea level.",
      "evidence": "European Southern Observatory (ESO) site specifications.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The maximum straight‑line distance at which the VLT could see a target of height 8.848 km from an altitude of 2.6 km is d = √(2 R h₁) + √(2 R h₂) ≈ 518 km (R≈6371 km).",
      "evidence": "Geometric horizon formula for two elevated points; calculation: √(2·6371·2.6)≈182 km, √(2·6371·8.848)≈336 km, sum≈518 km.",
      "weight": 0.45,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The great‑circle distance between Paranal (24.6° S, 70.4° W) and Mount Everest (27.99° N, 86.93° E) is about 17,700 km.",
      "evidence": "Haversine formula using the coordinates of the two sites.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the required distance (≈ 17,700 km) far exceeds the geometric line‑of‑sight limit (≈ 518 km), Earth’s curvature blocks any direct view of the mountain from the VLT.",
      "evidence": "If an object lies beyond the horizon distance, a straight line between observer and object intersects the Earth.",
      "weight": 0.07,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Even if a line‑of‑sight existed, the VLT’s optics are optimized for astronomical (effectively infinite) distances and cannot be refocused on a nearby terrestrial target without major redesign.",
      "evidence": "Technical manuals state VLT instruments are designed for objects at astronomical distances; focusing on a few‑kilometre target would require re‑configuring the collimation and adaptive‑optics system.",
      "weight": 0.03,
      "depends_on": [5]
    }
  ],
